#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436615 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(x{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,3)} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{1+{1}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1+81} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{82}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436642 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a16e4559960b923535cadecb1e623592c07fa11de5929e722cffb85679195a66.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $4,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})}^{3/4}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}({r}^{2})^{3/4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{3/2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}{r}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{2}{7}{r}^{7/2}]}_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(128-1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{254}{7}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{508\\pi }{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436855 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨2,-5,7⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨8,-2,-4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨8,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-5,7⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+(-4)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =16+10-28 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436856 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,0,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨7,-4,9⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-4,9⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 7+0\\cdot (-4)+4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436857 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}),$ if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨7,-2,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,4,2⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 7+4\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =7-8+6 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) &=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436858 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,5,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,3,2⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,5,4⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2+15+8 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436859 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-5,2,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨1,-5,4⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨-5,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-5,4⟩ \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436860 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨5,-8,-7⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,1,2⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-3,1,0⟩,$ find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(-1\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨5,-8,-7⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,-6,2⟩ \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)+(-8)\\cdot (-6)+(-7)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436861 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\hspace{0.167em}-1,\\hspace{0.167em}4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\hspace{0.167em}3,\\hspace{0.167em}-5⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨2\\sqrt{2},\\hspace{0.167em}-1,\\hspace{0.167em}4⟩\\cdot ⟨-2\\sqrt{2},\\hspace{0.167em}3,\\hspace{0.167em}-5⟩ \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2})(-2\\sqrt{2})+(-1)(3)+(4)(-5) \\\\  & =-8-3-20 \\\\  & =-31.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436862 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})$, if $5\\mathbf{a}\\cdot ((-4\\mathbf{b})\\times (-3\\mathbf{c}))=-120.$\n\n$\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a\\cdot ((-4b)\\times (-3c)) & =-120 \\\\ 5\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =-120 \\\\ 60[a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =-120 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\nb\\cdot (a\\times c)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436933 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=xyz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{y}^{3}{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nFind $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+2yz \\\\  & =3yz+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436934 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}={e}^{2x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-y{e}^{2x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-z{e}^{2x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ \nFind $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y{e}^{2x})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-z{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436951 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $\\pi $ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(\\pi ,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(\\pi ,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(\\pi ,1) & =(1){e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1) \\\\  & ={e}^{0}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436974 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 9\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/f0/f0a5627965f71f6eff869a45b1b575b892a67ebba9b1ec969f5dc38ba84cb250.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(0,-3).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +9 & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+6r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+6\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/75/7515fffda1580baa332df454e7fe53a60f70944215f13d83e0e2269a0101dba6.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =2\\pi $ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437016 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y,z)$ at $(1,-2,2)$ if $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2z-2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}z+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,2z-2xy,\\,2{x}^{2}z+2y⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-2,2) & =⟨2(1)(2)^{2}-(-2)^{2},\\,-2(1)(-2)+2(2),\\,2(1)^{2}(2)+2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-4,4+4,4-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,8,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437017 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇f(x,y)$ at $(e,1)$ if $f(x,y)={x}^{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}) \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\text{ }i+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{ }j, \\\\ ∇f(e,1) & =1(e)^{1-1}\\,i+{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\,j \\\\  & =i+e\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437018 :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $h(x,y)=y{e}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f\\,∇\\,h+h\\,∇\\,f\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y})={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})=y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j=y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =x{e}^{y}(y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x}\\text{ }j)+y{e}^{x}({e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j+y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+xy{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(x+1)y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437019 :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(1,-1)$ if $h(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}) \\\\  & =(2x+y){e}^{{x}^{2}+xy} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2x+y){e}^{{x}^{2}+xy},\\,x{e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-1) & =⟨(2(1)+(-1)){e}^{{1}^{2}+(1)(-1)},\\,1\\cdot {e}^{{1}^{2}+(1)(-1)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437020 :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =2x-2{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =4z-4yx \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =4y-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=2(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+4(z-yx)\\,j+4(y-z)\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+4(z-yx)\\,j+4(y-z)\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-{y}^{2} &=0, \\\\ z-yx &=0, \\\\ y-z &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe first equation gives $x={y}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain $z={y}^{3}.$ Substituting $z={y}^{3}$ into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=(-1)^{2}=1$ and $z=(-1)^{3}=-1.$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we obtain $x={0}^{2}=0$ and $z={0}^{3}=0.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ gives $x={1}^{2}=1$ and $z={1}^{3}=1.$\n\nFinally, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at three points: $(1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437021 :question/problem "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $\\mathrm{∇}h(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =y(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+2z{e}^{z}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =z(2+z){e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇h(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}y(x+1){e}^{x}=0 \\\\ x{e}^{x}=0 \\\\ z(2+z){e}^{z}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe second equation gives $x=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0+1){e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get  $z=0$ or $z=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437214 :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v \\\\ {u}^{2}{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x=u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v$ and $y={u}^{2}{v}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{u}{v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2{u}^{2}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡v & \\frac{u}{v} \\\\ 2u{v}^{2} & 2{u}^{2}v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡v\\cdot 2{u}^{2}v-\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot 2u{v}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡v-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437272 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x-y,$ where $x=-s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437273 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)\\operatorname{cos}x={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x+(-2\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x) \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x+2{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x \\\\  & =3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441615 :question/problem "![](images/23/23d35daa15e11899592683f2f3215506cc0337358be843780dbf1190c0e38e2c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 8,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{8}3{r}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{4}}{4}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{4}^{8} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (\\frac{3(8)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(4)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441616 :question/problem "![](images/82/823ab320e0f1426b846c7bf561b977ac6458c80d235aabdc5647a7db07c4cb74.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5}$ and $Q(x,y)=5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=10x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2x{y}^{3}+{x}^{5})\\,dx+(5{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}10x{y}^{2}-6x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =(8-2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right))-(2-0)\\cdot (0-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 3-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441617 :question/problem "![](images/df/df52e2add706a17bf93236cdaa04582932b6c799d4a88a3e194a3a3788c816ff.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+7x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3y$ and $Q(x,y)=7x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8\\pi  \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441618 :question/problem "![](images/69/69b42001c8ac5e95e18d89785e73fbba3e78554f9146e59bb50dd618b105f10c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=4y$ and $Q(x,y)=5x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}4y\\,dx+5x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 4}{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2}{2} \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442249 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac9f6016ee4c93aec58f8fff0a8002057f89d7c1bae589e5357a25709ae5a76c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{cos}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{sin}t-\\operatorname{sin}2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t+2\\operatorname{sin}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{cos}t-2\\operatorname{cos}2t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442250 :question/problem "![](images/bf/bfec7cbdb26a3db773ab1f6207de436ff0856b331cda183da530dbb6b91d91bd.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}t,\\hspace{0.222em}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}t)\\cdot (-3{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{3{\\operatorname{sin}}^{4}t{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442251 :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef44f01fb462cdf608f66a60dd11b8df798603bd204dca9d579ed50b370a84e3.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-\\operatorname{sin}4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{i}-4\\operatorname{cos}4t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}4t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\operatorname{cos}2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442252 :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a60729b626e1e9e219a839898cacc18aa81ec2a9b1b445e7b92b721874c2550.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{cos}t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445291 :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+7\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445292 :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445293 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445677 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that $$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\n$$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\text{ }\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{cos}t\\text{ }\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445678 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445679 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,i+xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,j+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=2t$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445680 :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,2t⟩$ at the point $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446239 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨3,-4⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨3,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{x+y}+xy{e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y(1+x){e}^{x+y}+2xy,\\,x(1+y){e}^{x+y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,-1) & =⟨(-1)\\cdot (1+1){e}^{1-1}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1),\\,1\\cdot (1-1){e}^{1-1}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446240 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$"}]}
